1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,062
Gemini's Flash model, the one every agent in this course was built against, prices

2
00:00:05,062 --> 00:00:09,659
out at roughly thirty cents per million words of input and two dollars fifty per

3
00:00:09,659 --> 00:00:11,039
million words of output.

4
00:00:11,039 --> 00:00:15,866
Priya's forty one daily emails do not come close to using a million of anything in a

5
00:00:15,866 --> 00:00:16,211
month.

6
00:00:16,211 --> 00:00:20,061
Why so cheap, when the technology is supposedly the expensive kind?

7
00:00:20,061 --> 00:00:24,888
Because you pay by the word, roughly: words the model reads, words it writes, priced

8
00:00:24,888 --> 00:00:28,566
separately, and an office task barely registers on either meter.

9
00:00:28,566 --> 00:00:32,876
An email is a couple of hundred words in; a triage verdict is one line out.

10
00:00:32,876 --> 00:00:37,588
You are buying judgement by the thimble, and a thimble of judgement, it turns out,

11
00:00:37,588 --> 00:00:39,140
costs a fraction of a cent.

12
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:43,679
The intuition that AI must be expensive comes from what it costs to build these

13
00:00:43,679 --> 00:00:44,082
models.

14
00:00:44,082 --> 00:00:48,909
Renting one, per call, at office volumes, is one of the cheapest inputs your job has

15
00:00:48,909 --> 00:00:49,426
ever had.

16
00:00:49,976 --> 00:00:51,995
That number is not a rounding trick.

17
00:00:51,995 --> 00:00:56,483
Small, short, structured tasks like triage and extraction are exactly what these

18
00:00:56,483 --> 00:00:57,773
models are cheapest at.

19
00:00:57,773 --> 00:01:02,260
The bill that actually adds up belongs to a different kind of workload entirely:

20
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:06,524
long documents, high volume, or a model far more capable than the job needs.

21
00:01:06,524 --> 00:01:10,001
Know what would actually move the number, so surprises cannot.

22
00:01:10,001 --> 00:01:14,433
Feed the agent fifty page policy documents instead of emails and the words-read

23
00:01:14,433 --> 00:01:15,723
meter starts mattering.

24
00:01:15,723 --> 00:01:20,379
Point it at a shared inbox with ten times the volume and the arithmetic scales with

25
00:01:20,379 --> 00:01:21,444
it, still modestly.

26
00:01:21,444 --> 00:01:25,932
Swap the small fast model for the largest one out of vague ambition and the rate

27
00:01:25,932 --> 00:01:30,644
itself jumps, which is why Section 2.2's surgeon-opening-the-post warning was also a

28
00:01:30,644 --> 00:01:31,485
budgeting rule.

29
00:01:31,485 --> 00:01:36,029
None of those describe your four agents, which is exactly why the estimate holds.

30
00:01:36,579 --> 00:01:40,788
The comparison is where the value actually lives, so make it out loud when you

31
00:01:40,788 --> 00:01:41,922
present your numbers.

32
00:01:41,922 --> 00:01:46,186
Paying only for what ran, at rates you can audit any morning, is an unglamorous

33
00:01:46,186 --> 00:01:50,557
superpower for anyone who has ever had to justify a software line item, and it is

34
00:01:50,557 --> 00:01:54,713
the difference between asking a manager for a budget and telling them about a

35
00:01:54,713 --> 00:01:55,523
rounding error.

36
00:01:56,073 --> 00:02:00,673
This is exactly why the course keeps tool-specific facts like this one quarantined

37
00:02:00,673 --> 00:02:04,263
to lectures like this, instead of scattered through every build.

38
00:02:04,263 --> 00:02:06,732
The judgement spine survives a price change.

39
00:02:06,732 --> 00:02:08,583
This one number does not need to.

40
00:02:08,583 --> 00:02:12,622
The re-check, when you need it, is a sixty second errand with two stops.

41
00:02:12,622 --> 00:02:15,764
Your provider's pricing page tells you the current rate.

42
00:02:15,764 --> 00:02:20,420
Your own usage dashboard, on the same account where you made the API key, tells you

43
00:02:20,420 --> 00:02:25,020
what last month actually cost, which is the only number that ever needs defending.

44
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:29,621
Do that once a quarter, when the freshness pass on your own materials comes round,

45
00:02:29,621 --> 00:02:33,660
and pricing news between checks becomes something you can safely ignore.

46
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:38,260
Headlines about AI costs are written about training runs and enterprise contracts,

47
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:40,392
not about nine hundred emails a month.

48
00:02:40,392 --> 00:02:44,487
Your dashboard is the only pricing journalism your agents will ever need.

49
00:02:45,037 --> 00:02:49,412
Keep that ranking in mind for the rest of this section, because it inverts where

50
00:02:49,412 --> 00:02:51,162
most people put their attention.

51
00:02:51,162 --> 00:02:55,537
The model bill is the number everyone asks about and the one that matters least:

52
00:02:55,537 --> 00:02:57,396
rounding error against any salary.

53
00:02:57,396 --> 00:03:01,990
Your checking time is the real cost of running agents, which is why this course made

54
00:03:01,990 --> 00:03:06,474
you count it from the first trial, and why the next lecture's tracker subtracts it

55
00:03:06,474 --> 00:03:08,552
before claiming a single saved minute.

56
00:03:08,552 --> 00:03:10,685
Optimise the hours, tolerate the cents.

57
00:03:10,685 --> 00:03:15,005
Next lecture turns that into a document you fill in with your own real numbers.
